# Simple forecasting methods {#sec-benchmarks data-tr-id="ub1f0f9" data-tr-status="missing" data-tr-reviews="0" lang="en"}

[Some forecasting methods are extremely simple and yet surprisingly effective.
They are mostly used as benchmark methods: any new method should do better
than these before it is worth using.]{.tr data-tr-id="u886e23" data-tr-status="missing" data-tr-reviews="0" lang="en"}

## Naive and seasonal naive {#sec-naive data-tr-id="u3fd307" data-tr-status="missing" data-tr-reviews="0" lang="en"}

[The **naive** method uses the last observation as the forecast for every
future period. The **seasonal naive** method instead uses the value from the
same season of the last year, so a forecast for next July is this July's value.]{.tr data-tr-id="u6e75d2" data-tr-status="missing" data-tr-reviews="0" lang="en"}


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
h <- 24  # forecast horizon in months
last_year <- tail(as.numeric(AirPassengers), 12)

# Repeat the final year of data to cover the horizon
forecast_values <- rep(last_year, length.out = h)
future <- seq(as.Date("1961-01-01"), by = "month", length.out = h)

message(sprintf("Produced %d forecasts from %d observations", h, length(AirPassengers)))
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stderr}

```
Produced 24 forecasts from 144 observations
```


:::

```{.r .cell-code}
plot(future, forecast_values, type = "l",
     main = "Seasonal naive forecasts",
     xlab = "Month", ylab = "Passengers (thousands)")
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Seasonal naive forecasts for the next two years.](benchmarks_files/figure-html/fig-snaive-1.png){#fig-snaive width=672}
:::
:::


[Because the series has a strong trend, the seasonal naive forecasts in
@fig-snaive are probably too low. A method that also models the trend would
do better.]{.tr data-tr-id="ua8630e" data-tr-status="missing" data-tr-reviews="0" lang="en"}

## Measuring uncertainty {#sec-uncertainty data-tr-id="ubc1e52" data-tr-status="missing" data-tr-reviews="0" lang="en"}

[A single number hides how uncertain a forecast is. A prediction interval gives
a range in which the future value is expected to lie with a stated
probability, such as 80% or 95%. Intervals usually become wider as the
forecast horizon increases, because uncertainty accumulates over time.]{.tr data-tr-id="u6cf767" data-tr-status="missing" data-tr-reviews="0" lang="en"}
